WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.830
A multi-GPU AI system uses more than one graphics processor for compute.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.635
The GPUs may live in one workstation,

00:00:06.635 --> 00:00:09.470
connect through supported external or server designs,

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.023
or operate in separate machines coordinated by software.

00:00:12.023 --> 00:00:15.620
Gaming multi-GPU historically focused on rendering frames.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.335
Compute multi-GPU divides numerical work or independent jobs.

00:00:19.335 --> 00:00:23.240
Language-model inference may split one model, place different models on different devices,

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.210
or route separate requests to separate GPUs.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:29.770
Multiple GPUs do not automatically behave like one simple larger GPU.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:34.055
Each device has its own memory, compute, thermal limits, and connection path.

00:00:34.055 --> 00:00:37.275
Software must explicitly decide how model data and work move among them.

00:00:37.275 --> 00:00:41.910
One use case is capacity: fitting a model or context that does not fit on one device.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.880
Another is throughput: processing more independent jobs or users.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.410
A third is pipeline separation,

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:50.495
such as placing vision encoding and text generation on different resources.

00:00:50.495 --> 00:00:52.696
These goals have different architectures and metrics.

00:00:52.696 --> 00:00:55.875
Splitting one request may add synchronization and transfer delay.

00:00:55.875 --> 00:00:59.680
Running independent requests can scale more cleanly but does not help one oversized model

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:00.495
fit.

00:01:00.495 --> 00:01:04.420
Multi-GPU is worthwhile when a measured workload justifies power, heat, cost,

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.000
configuration, monitoring, and failure complexity.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.040
Start by stating whether the requirement is larger-model capacity, single-request speed,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.600
concurrency, batch throughput, or experimentation.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.120
Also define the failure domain.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.280
One split model may stop when either GPU fails,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.419
while separate model replicas may preserve partial service.

00:01:20.419 --> 00:01:23.700
More devices increase options, but architecture determines resilience.
